    {
      "id": "hook",
      "title": "hook (how many fundamental particles?)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "So far we've got two quarks, a few leptons, their antimatter partners, and some forces. How many kinds of truly fundamental particle do you think there are? Make your prediction. In the Standard Model, it's seventeen kinds. So how does the Standard Model organise particles and interactions?"
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 3,
      "holdKind": "countdown",
      "planningResponseInterval": {
        "countdownSeconds": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "two-groups",
      "title": "two kinds of thing",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The Standard Model is our best theory of the fundamental particles, and three of the four forces between them. It sorts everything into two groups. There are matter particles, the quarks and leptons, which make things up. And there are force carriers, which pass forces between them."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "quarks",
      "title": "six quarks",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Start with the quarks. There are six types, called flavours, in three pairs. Up and down, charm and strange, top and bottom. In every pair, the first has a charge of plus two thirds, and the second minus one third. Each pair, called a generation, is a heavier copy of the one before. Ordinary matter uses only up and down. The others are made in high-energy collisions, and decay almost straight away. The top quark has about the mass of a whole gold atom, yet as far as we know, it has no parts."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "leptons",
      "title": "six leptons",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the leptons. The electron, the muon and the tau all have a charge of minus one. Each has its own neutrino, with no charge at all. Same three generations, and the charged leptons get heavier each time. The tau is nearly twice as heavy as a proton, but it's still a lepton, because it has no parts and doesn't feel the strong force."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "antimatter",
      "title": "antimatter for everything",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Every one of these has an antimatter partner, with the same mass. People often say opposite charge, but it's more than that. All its charge-like labels are reversed, not only electric charge. That's why an antineutron, with no charge, is still different from a neutron. Its count of baryons is minus one, not plus one. So an anti-up quark has a charge of minus two thirds, and an anti-down has plus one third."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "hadron-charges",
      "title": "adding up hadrons",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now we can build hadrons and check their charges. A pion plus is an up and an anti-down. Plus two thirds, plus one third, makes plus one. A pion minus is a down and an anti-up, which makes minus one. An antiproton is two anti-ups and an anti-down, which makes minus one too. Quarks have fractional charges, but every hadron ever found adds up to a whole number."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "four-forces",
      "title": "four forces",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the interactions. There are four fundamental forces. The strong force is the strongest, and it acts on quarks. Its leftover pull between protons and neutrons reaches only about the width of a nucleus. The electromagnetic force acts on charged particles, and reaches forever. The weak force is weaker, reaches even less far than the strong force, and acts on all quarks and leptons, even neutrinos. Gravity is by far the weakest, but it acts on everything and reaches forever. It wins on big scales, because it only attracts, while positive and negative charges cancel out."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "carriers",
      "title": "forces as exchanges",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "In the Standard Model, a force happens when particles exchange a carrier. Picture two boats on a lake, passing a heavy bag from one to the other. Each boat recoils, so they drift apart. In the same way, two electrons repel by exchanging photons. The strong force is carried by gluons, between quarks. The weak force is carried by the W and Z bosons."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "range",
      "title": "why some forces are short",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Why is the weak force so short-ranged? Its carriers are heavy, about eighty-five to a hundred times a proton's mass. The heavier the exchanged carrier, the shorter the distance the force can reach. The photon has no mass, so electromagnetism reaches forever. Gluons have no mass either, but they're confined, trapped with the quarks inside protons and neutrons, so the strong force is short-ranged too."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "weak-flavour",
      "title": "the force that changes quarks",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The weak force does something no other force can. It changes one type of quark into another. In beta minus decay, a down quark inside a neutron turns into an up quark, giving out a W minus. The W minus then becomes an electron and an antineutrino. That's why the Sun burns slowly. The first step of its fusion needs a weak interaction, to turn a proton into a neutron."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "higgs",
      "title": "the Higgs",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "One more piece. The Higgs boson was discovered in twenty twelve, at CERN, with a mass of about a hundred and twenty-five GeV. It shows that the Higgs field is real, and that field gives the W and Z, the quarks and the charged leptons their mass. It doesn't give mass to the photon or the gluons. And here's a surprise. Most of a proton's mass doesn't come from the Higgs at all. It comes from the energy of the quarks and gluons moving inside it."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "incomplete",
      "title": "what's missing",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "So the Standard Model organises nearly everything, and it has passed every test so far. But it isn't complete. Gravity isn't in it at all. Dark matter, which outweighs ordinary matter in galaxies, isn't any particle in the table. And neutrinos turned out to have mass, which the original model left out. That's how we know our understanding of matter is still incomplete."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "memory-aid",
      "title": "a way to remember",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's a way to remember the whole table. Six quarks, six leptons, four carriers, one Higgs."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 2,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "attempt-1",
      "title": "attempt 1 (countdown, 10 seconds)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Your turn. A K minus meson is made of a strange quark and an anti-up quark. Calculate its charge, state whether it's a baryon or a meson, and give the quark content of its antiparticle. Pause here to answer."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 10,
      "holdKind": "countdown",
      "planningResponseInterval": {
        "countdownSeconds": 10
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "feedback-1",
      "title": "feedback 1 (brief)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "The strange quark gives minus one third. The anti-up gives minus two thirds, so the total is minus one. It's a quark and an antiquark, so it's a meson. Its antiparticle swaps each one, an anti-strange and an up, which is the K plus. The slip is giving the anti-up plus two thirds, which gives an impossible fractional charge."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "attempt-2",
      "title": "attempt 2 (countdown, 10 seconds)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Last one. For each process, identify the fundamental force and its carrier. Beta minus decay, quarks held together in a proton, and two electrons repelling. Pause here to answer."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 10,
      "holdKind": "countdown",
      "planningResponseInterval": {
        "countdownSeconds": 10
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "feedback-2",
      "title": "feedback 2 (brief)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Beta decay changes a down quark into an up, so it's the weak force, carried by the W minus boson. Quarks are held together by the strong force, carried by gluons. Electrons repel through the electromagnetic force, carried by photons. The slip is calling beta decay the strong force, just because it happens in a nucleus. Only the weak force changes quark type."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 1,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "handoff-v22",
      "title": "handoff",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Look at the table again. Most of it never turns up in ordinary matter. Charm, strange, top and bottom, the muon and the tau, the W, the Z and the Higgs all had to be made, and then caught, before anyone could test the theory. So how do accelerators create and test evidence about particles?"
      },
